  "reasoning_content": "We need answer user's request: explain how relational database executes SQL query, from parsing and planning through indexing, joins, transactions, and returning rows. Detailed technical tutorial with concrete examples and tradeoffs, including common performance pitfalls. Use complete sentences and avoid repeating yourself.\n\nNeed produce final answer. Must be thorough. Need likely structure: overview pipeline: tokenizer/parser, binder/resolver, optimizer (logical/physical), executor, storage engine, concurrency control, network. Include examples SQL: orders, customers, products. Discuss parse, syntax tree, name resolution, type coercion, view expansion, authorization. Planning: logical operations, predicate pushdown, join reordering, access paths, cardinality estimates, stats, histograms, cost model (CPU, I/O, memory), dynamic vs static, adaptive. Physical operators: seq scan, index scan, bitmap heap scan, table scan, nested loop, hash join, sort merge join, index merge, materialize, aggregation, window. Indexes: B-tree, hash, GiST/GIN? Focus relational; primary keys, unique, composite order, selectivity, covering, partial, expression, write amplification, maintenance, index-only scans, index scans vs seq. SQL examples: exact match, range, IN, prefix. Index order matters: composite (customer_id, order_date) vs (order_date, customer_id); leftmost prefix, range stops. Multi-tenancy. Hash indexes? Memory. Bitmap. Join types and algorithms. Example: SELECT c.name, sum(o.total) FROM orders o JOIN customers c ON o.customer_id=c.id WHERE o.status='paid' GROUP BY c.name; optimizer choose hash join vs nested loop. Tradeoffs: NLJ for small inner or indexed lookups, hash for large builds, sort merge for sorted inputs or external memory. Outer joins and anti/semi. Subqueries: semi-join, materialization. Transactions: isolation levels, MVCC, locks, snapshots, serializable SSI, write skew, deadlock, WAL, commit durability, read committed vs repeatable. How query interacts: read view, visibility, buffer manager, dirty pages, fsync. Pitfalls: N+1 in app, correlated subqueries, SELECT *, missing index, bad join order, cross join, implicit conversion, functions on indexed column, low-cardinality filter, overuse of indexes, outdated stats, temp tables, LIMIT with bad order by, count(*) over huge, group by memory spills, lock contention, unbounded joins, fan-out, missing",
